{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Come near to me, I pray you. And they came near.</b> He showed them that he was circumcised. By this he wished to demonstrate his righteousness, that he had been among those steeped in immorality and did not stumble in forbidden relations, for one who lies with an Aramit draws his foreskin back over himself (Eruvin 19a). And we find (in Midrash Tehillim 114:4) that Israel were redeemed from Egypt in the merit of four things: they did not change their names, they did not change their language, there were no slanderers among them, and they were guarded from forbidden relations. And the reason for this will be explained in parashat Shemot (Exodus 1:1) with the help of HaShem. And similarly here, when Joseph sent for his father and his brothers, he informed them through what merit they would go up from there, and he alluded to them these four in which Joseph was complete in all of them: that he did not change his name — for he said to them, ״אֲנִי יוֹסֵף״ (Genesis 45:3), even though Pharaoh called his name Tzaphenat Paneach; nevertheless, ״אֲנִי יוֹסֵף״ — this is my name forever. And that he did not change his language — for he said, ״כִּי פִי הַמְדַבֵּר אֲלֵיכֶם״ (Genesis 45:12), in the holy tongue. And that he was not given to license in forbidden relations — as it says, ״גְּשׁוּ נָא אֵלַי,״ he showed them that he was circumcised. And that he was not a slanderer — as it says, ״גְּשׁוּ אֵלַי,״ for he did not want Benjamin his brother to hear the matter of the recognition, and even to his father he told him nothing at all, for had he revealed it, he would certainly have commanded him before his death, ״שָׂא נָא פֶּשַׁע אַחֶיךָ״ (Genesis 50:17). And Joseph alluded to them that on account of the sin of the recognition they would pay the debt of ״כִּי גֵר יִהְיֶה זַרְעֲךָ״ etc. (Genesis 15:13), as it is written, ״וְעַתָּה אַל תֵּעָצְבוּ״ (Genesis 45:5) — precisely now, but in the future you shall grieve. Therefore he alluded to them also the redemption and the things that bring about the redemption. And some say ״וְעַתָּה״ alluded to the ten martyrs of the kingdom, who in the future will grieve over the recognition."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:30.438584076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}